**Read:** [Quantum Mechanics — David Tong](../sources/Quantum-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_0GY6BHCRA9CR5YGBY1AT9DRZY0), sections 1.1 and 3.1–3.2, returning through the full chapters as the next steps develop.

Write z=a+ib, its conjugate a−ib and |z|²=a²+b². For a finite vector, the inner product conjugates the first factor. Normalize (1,i) by √2 and distinguish its components from their squared magnitudes. A global phase leaves probabilities unchanged; a relative phase can change an interference measurement.

An eigenvector satisfies Hv=Ev. Begin with the real symmetric matrix [[Δ,t],[t,−Δ]] and derive E=±√(Δ²+t²). Verify orthogonality and normalization, including the separate degenerate case Δ=t=0. This small example prepares band calculations without presuming an entire completed algebra curriculum.

**Work:** Calculate an expectation and variance in a superposition, then change the basis and recover the same observable. Explain why reading an amplitude directly as a probability can give an impossible result. For each new differential or matrix operator, name its domain and boundary conditions.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
